# node4 install identity — convenience tier, never security-critical.

BOX=node4
ID_SCHEME=z23-install-v1
INSTALL_ID=493df36a56e5064f42d7dc1f814994c9002af9feabcb2d96e7c5424693c59a7c
MACHINE_TAG=cc2549ef97802a87dc856a3309ab544b90aa5f6b086574862158513c2d2b35b2
BOOT_TAG=2b335ff62a74dc1f43674fe0c015693058710e22cc6602f594cef64b75d41c42
CLAIM=identifies-this-installation-only-not-hardware
OBSERVED=2026-08-25

# Every value above is sha256(SALT || raw) with the salt held only in the
# operator env. Raw host keys and machine-id are never committed: a raw
# hostkey hash would let anyone with internet scan data match a scanned
# address to this box, which is exactly what committing onions avoids.
#
# RECIPE for INSTALL_ID, for a reader who holds the salt:
#   { printf "%s\\n" "$ZCL_FLEET_ID_SALT"; \
#     cat /etc/ssh/ssh_host_*.pub | awk "NF>=2 {print \$1, \$2}" \
#       | LC_ALL=C sort | sha256sum | cut -d" " -f1; } | sha256sum
# The trailing key comment is dropped on purpose: renaming the host must
# not move the name of the installation.
#
# No address appears here, per the devfleet privacy rule. Where the box
# answers is reachability, not identity, and lives in the operator env.
#
# BOOT_TAG changes on every reboot by design. It is recorded to explain
# heartbeat gaps and is NOT part of the pin.
#
# METAL_TIER is an optional owner-gated field that is absent here, which is
# normal. Nothing reads for it, nothing waits on it.
